Post-check proof for makeLoft (P11-FEAT-009): what happens when the adapter uses OCCT's default
ThruSections settings (smooth interpolation, the kernel's own compatibility matching) instead of
BetterCAD's (ruled, CheckCompatibility(false) on BetterCAD's matching).
Captured 2026-09-15T22:25:15+10:00 on the Debug build with this two-line change in makeLoft():

-        BRepOffsetAPI_ThruSections loft(/*isSolid=*/true, /*ruled=*/true);
+        BRepOffsetAPI_ThruSections loft(/*isSolid=*/true, /*ruled=*/false);
-        loft.CheckCompatibility(false);
+        loft.CheckCompatibility(true);

Command: build/debug/bin/bettercad_tests.exe "[loft]" --reporter compact
Result: 13 of the 48 loft test cases fail; no wrong solid is committed:
  - smooth interpolation through three or more sections: refused by the prismatoid volume check
    (22514.7 against 18325.96 mm^3; 9965.9 against 9361.9 mm^3), so the features fail and export nothing;
  - the kernel re-matching sections itself (CheckCompatibility(true)): refused by the same check
    (6164.4 against 7717.8 mm^3), or by the self-interference check;
  - the 45 deg square tie: with the kernel's own matching the twist flips under 1e-12 rad of noise
    (Loft_BreaksTiesDeterministically), which BetterCAD's tie rule prevents.
The change was then reverted; OcctSweeps.cpp was checked by SHA-256:
58336d2c01a3c0e8ec3d4a65361147beec435debf3943f26cdffd193aa0a60ea

